WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.799 --> 00:00:03.020
Hi everyone, and welcome to Biology Professor.

00:00:03.020 --> 00:00:06.240
Today, we're talking about the Law of Segregation.

00:00:06.240 --> 00:00:11.900
Now the Law of Segregation is one of the laws
of Mendelian Inheritance.

00:00:11.900 --> 00:00:18.359
These talk about inheritance of biological
features, which we now call genes.

00:00:18.359 --> 00:00:29.270
And the inheritance of genes follows three
Laws for the pattern of biological inheritance.

00:00:29.270 --> 00:00:34.199
These laws were first proposed by Gregor Mendel
in the 1860's.

00:00:34.199 --> 00:00:42.920
He was working with pea plants.

00:00:42.920 --> 00:00:48.949
And it was his work with pea plants that allowed
him to elucidate three different laws of inheritance.

00:00:48.949 --> 00:00:54.600
These are the Law of Segregation, which is
what we are talking about today, also the

00:00:54.600 --> 00:00:57.460
Law of Independent Assortment and the Law
of Dominance.

00:00:57.460 --> 00:01:02.129
If you are interested in learning more about
these other two Laws, see my videos on those

00:01:02.129 --> 00:01:03.129
topics.

00:01:03.129 --> 00:01:05.080
But now for the Law of Segregation.

00:01:05.080 --> 00:01:08.780
It deals with gamete formation.

00:01:08.780 --> 00:01:21.260
Remember that gametes refer to the cells produced
by parents that later unite to form offspring.

00:01:21.260 --> 00:01:27.590
In humans, these are sperm cells from the
father and egg cells from the mother.

00:01:27.590 --> 00:01:34.350
And when sperm cells and egg cells are made,
you have alleles for each genes, remember

00:01:34.350 --> 00:01:42.240
that alleles are just alternative forms of
a gene, so in a plant you may have a gene

00:01:42.240 --> 00:01:48.550
for flower color with one allele from the
mother and one allele from the father.

00:01:48.550 --> 00:01:52.560
Perhaps purple flowers from the mother, white
flowers from the father.

00:01:52.560 --> 00:01:58.590
That's an example of two different alleles,
two different alternative forms for the same

00:01:58.590 --> 00:02:00.560
gene, flower color.

00:02:00.560 --> 00:02:14.810
Now, during gamete formation, alleles for
each gene segregate from each other.

00:02:14.810 --> 00:02:19.570
This is just another way of saying that they
separate from each other in some way.

00:02:19.570 --> 00:02:24.730
This means that each gamete carries only one
allele per gene.

00:02:24.730 --> 00:02:31.900
So, for example, if we have a man here - remember
that humans are diploid, so they have two

00:02:31.900 --> 00:02:37.990
copies of each chromosome, that's why we call
it 2n - so here we have our little man, he

00:02:37.990 --> 00:02:46.569
is heterozygous - remember that heterozygous
means having two different alleles for a gene,

00:02:46.569 --> 00:02:53.010
so in this example, with a man who has a dominant
allele and a recessive allele that he got

00:02:53.010 --> 00:03:00.319
from his parents, and in gamete formation,
each gamete will have one of those alleles,

00:03:00.319 --> 00:03:03.730
so either the dominant one or the recessive
one.

00:03:03.730 --> 00:03:10.780
Of course, if this man had been homozygous,
meaning having two identical copies of an

00:03:10.780 --> 00:03:15.510
allele, every single one of his gametes would
have that allele.

00:03:15.510 --> 00:03:19.540
So the gametes are 1n, or haploid.

00:03:19.540 --> 00:03:23.010
This is because they only have one set of
chromosomes.

00:03:23.010 --> 00:03:30.430
So this is what the Law of Segregation says,
and when these alleles segregate from each

00:03:30.430 --> 00:03:41.120
other, it's happening during the process known
as meiosis.

00:03:41.120 --> 00:03:47.440
And then once the gametes have been formed
during the process of meiosis, they will then

00:03:47.440 --> 00:03:57.319
randomly unite 
in the process of fertilization.

00:03:57.319 --> 00:04:02.740
And this is where a lot of our genetic variety comes from.

00:04:02.740 --> 00:04:09.569
The fact that the alleles segregate from each other, that the gametes can have different

00:04:09.569 --> 00:04:16.310
combination of what the parent has, and the fact that you have one random sperm uniting

00:04:16.310 --> 00:04:20.879
with one random egg, this results in a lot
of potential diversity.

00:04:20.879 --> 00:04:26.340
This is why no two people are alike, no two siblings are alike.

00:04:26.340 --> 00:04:32.599
And it all has to do with the Law of Segregation,
thanks for watching.

00:04:32.599 --> 00:04:36.650
Remember my other two videos on the Law of
Independent Assortment and the Law of Dominance

00:04:36.650 --> 00:04:38.362
if you are interested in learning more about
those.

00:04:38.362 --> 00:04:41.599
And we will see you here at Biology Professor
next time.

